# Cloudflare Pages routing. First matching rule wins, so order in this file matters.
#
# The app is a client-rendered SPA with hand-rolled path routing (src/App.tsx), so every
# app route has to be served the same shell. Pages does that by default for ANY unmatched
# path, which is the problem: a typo, a mangled pasted link, or a probe all got the shell
# with a 200, so the whole domain was one infinite set of valid-looking pages. Google found
# "https://sportydolphin.fun/wpbl),and" that way and indexed it as a third page of the site.
#
# So the fallback is inverted here: the app's routes are listed explicitly and rewritten to
# the shell with a 200, and everything else falls through to the catch-all at the bottom
# and gets a real 404. That makes this list load-bearing. ADD A LINE IN BOTH BLOCKS WHEN
# YOU ADD A ROUTE to the `Route` union in src/App.tsx, or the new page 404s in production
# while working perfectly under `npm run dev` (Vite serves the shell for everything).
#
# The rewrite target is `/`, NOT `/index.html`. Pages canonicalizes `/index.html` to `/`
# with a 308, so naming the file here makes every app route a redirect to the home page
# instead of a rewrite. It serves the same asset either way; only the spelling is safe.

# Trailing-slash variants, folded onto the canonical form. Pages serves both spellings
# otherwise, which is a duplicate of every page for a crawler to sort out. 301, not a
# rewrite: there should be one URL per page and this says which one it is.
/wpbl/           /wpbl            301
/wpbl/api/       /wpbl/api        301
/wpbl/schedule/  /wpbl/schedule   301
/wpbl/standings/ /wpbl/standings  301
/wpbl/stats/     /wpbl/stats      301
/wpbl/teams/     /wpbl/teams      301
/wpbl/players/   /wpbl/players    301
/mlb/          /mlb       301
/privacy/      /privacy   301
/terms/        /terms     301
/admin/        /admin     301
/cups/         /cups      301
/stopwatch/    /stopwatch 301
/weights/      /weights   301
/poop/         /poop      301
/testgame/     /testgame  301

# App routes: rewritten to the shell, status 200, URL unchanged in the address bar (which
# is what the router and the canonical tags in src/seo.ts both expect).
/wpbl            /  200
/wpbl/api        /  200
# The section's tabs. Listed one by one rather than as /wpbl/* on purpose: a wildcard would
# hand a 200 to every mistyped path under the section, which is the exact soft-404 hole this
# file exists to close.
/wpbl/schedule   /  200
/wpbl/standings  /  200
/wpbl/stats      /  200
/wpbl/teams      /  200
/wpbl/players    /  200
# The one wildcard in this file, and the exception that proves the rule. A player URL is
# /wpbl/players/<slug>, and the set of valid slugs lives in the database, so it cannot be
# enumerated here. The soft-404 hole it would otherwise open is closed one layer up instead:
# functions/wpbl/[[tab]].ts resolves the slug against the roster and answers a real 404 for
# anything that names nobody, BEFORE this rule is ever reached. Delete that check and every
# typo under /wpbl/players/ silently becomes an indexable page again.
/wpbl/players/*  /  200
/mlb           /  200
/privacy       /  200
/terms         /  200
/admin         /  200
/cups          /  200
/stopwatch     /  200
/weights       /  200
/poop          /  200
/testgame      /  200

# Everything else. Static files (assets, icons, robots.txt, sitemap.xml) and the Pages
# Functions in functions/ are both matched ahead of this rule, so it only ever catches
# paths that genuinely do not exist. Verified with `npx wrangler pages dev dist`.
/*             /404.html  404
